    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-37",
      "problem": "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=x^3$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty } x^3 = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
